 ],
 "notes": "26 stations, nos. 4-29, in printed order. Headings as printed: 'East Coast of Scotland.' (line 212, nos. 4-7, which are the Shetland lights; no 'Shetland' is printed), 'Orkney.' (line 222), 'Main Land.' (line 232).\n\nFragments are whole lines copied exactly, with leading spaces kept. Names keep their printed punctuation, such as trailing full stops and the comma before a place. Italic underscores are left out of name/place and recorded as 'italic' in marks.\n\nHeights: the column header says 'Height of centre of light above water.' Only row 4 prints 'ft.' ('280 ft.'). The other rows give bare numbers. I put them in other.\n\nFootnote marks († ‡ § ‖ ¶) are not return marks, so they are not in marks. Where a mark is attached to a keeper's name (Wm. Anderson†, John Tulloch.‡, Alexander Harp.§), the keeper's name is clean and the mark is noted in other. Where a mark is attached to a height (346‖ Dunnet Head, 224¶ St. Abb's Head), it is kept as printed in other.\n\nFootnotes (lines 250-269):\n- † Records after 22 Nov. were filled in by John Wilson. Before that, Wm. Anderson filled them in; he has since been transferred to St. Abb's Head.\n- ‡ North Ronaldshay returned no schedule, only a note on Swans in a letter. So the ** there is questionable.\n- § The Hoy Sound (Low) schedule said nothing of importance was seen in 1880.\n- ‖ Dunnet Head lost its observer, Mr. George Maclachlan, who left. He is a keeper named only in a footnote, not in the row.\n- ¶ No returns had ever been received from St. Abb's Head.\n\nSumburgh Head: a brace '}' joins the two keepers' lines (Wm. Anderson† and / John Wilson.). I recorded it in other, not as a station-grouping brace.\n\nMark contradictions:\n- Dunnet Head (15) and Noss Head (17) print both '*' (returns received) and italics (meaning returns last year but none this year). The two cannot both apply. The ‖ footnote suggests the observer left.\n- Girdleness (24) is italic, though only the name is in italics, not ', Aberdeen.'.\n- No † 'new this year' prefix appears anywhere in this section.\n\nCopied as printed, but possibly wrong:\n- 'Whalsey Skerries' (usually Whalsay)\n- 'Neven Kerr' (Nevin?)\n- 'North Ronaldshay' (no full stop, unlike the others)\n- 'Montroseness, Aberdeen.' (Montrose/Scurdie Ness is in Forfarshire)\n- 'Chanonry Point, Elgin.' (it is on the Black Isle, Ross-shire; compare the earlier finding that Chanonry was a slip in the 1879 print)\n- 'Cromarty, East Cromarty.'\n- 'Inch Keith' (two words)\n- 'Robert Grierson' (no full stop)\n\nNo light characters or types (L.H./L.V.) are printed in this section.",
